<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanem–Bornu Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanem-Bornu, Kanem–Bornu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a> was a long-lived state around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, first centred in Kanem and later in Bornu. It was ruled for centuries by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> and was among the earliest states in the region to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. It has its own profile on the site and reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-alooma">Idris Alooma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire.jpg" alt="Kanem–Bornu Empire" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Megartonius · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanem%E2%80%93Bornu,_13th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a> was based around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> and at various times ruled areas that are now parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. Its early history is poorly known. It is believed to have been founded around the year 700, though later and earlier dates have been proposed, in the lands between Lake Chad and the Bahr el Ghazal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duguwa-dynasty">Duguwa dynasty</a> ruled from the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njimi">Njimi</a> in Kanem and used the title mai. The empire was sustained by the trans-Saharan trade. The name Kanem–Bornu is a modern historians' name, an amalgamation of the empire's two central regions, and not a name the state used itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 11th century the empire converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duguwa-dynasty">Duguwa</a>. The mai Dunama II Dibalemi brought it to its height in the 13th century. It controlled Saharan trade routes and exported salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, slaves and animal products. In the 14th century economic factors and conflict with the Bilala led to the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, and Umar I Idrismi re-centred the empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>. After a century of instability the mai Ali I Gaji founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngazargamu">Ngazargamu</a>, around 1472, as a permanent capital. The empire reached its peak under Idris IV Alooma in the late 16th century and began a slow decline in the 17th.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> jihads of the early 19th century nearly ended it and moved the centre of regional power west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. The al-Kanemi dynasty, ruling as shehus, replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa</a>. In 1893 the Sudanese slave raider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> armies and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukawa">Kukawa</a>, which was plundered and burned, its 120,000 to 200,000 inhabitants killed, enslaved or dispersed. The al-Kanemi dynasty was restored in 1900 under colonial suzerainty, and in 1902 the territories were absorbed into the French, British and German colonial empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The mai was variously equated to an emperor or a king, and after the conversion rulers were also called sultan and emir. Any son of a mai could succeed, which led to succession conflicts and civil wars, and hardly any mai was fully secure. Perhaps in the 13th century the empire moved to a feudal system with a class of princes and a large system of titles. The mai held audience from a wooden cage called the zanadir. All land belonged formally to the sovereign, who granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> under the chima system to courtiers, princes, princesses and clerics. Most fiefs returned to the ruler at the holder's death. The Borno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dikwa">Dikwa</a> emirates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> survive as remnants of the regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanem–Bornu Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
